    "recommendations": "Reducing vegetation density immediately surrounding the structure and creating greater separation between shrubs, tree clusters, and the building perimeter could improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity near the improvements. Where heavy brush and scattered shrubs exist in close proximity to the structure, thinning and reducing those concentrations may lower the opportunity for fire to move directly toward the building. Managing lower branches on scattered trees near the structure could further reduce vertical fuel continuity. Maintaining cleared buffers along the single access route is particularly important, as unobstructed ingress and egress would support firefighter access and safe evacuation. Given the brushland interface conditions and limited existing defensible space, consistent and ongoing vegetation management within the area immediately surrounding the structure and along the driveway corridor should be considered a priority for long-term wildfire resilience and property preparedness."
